    {
      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment of an OIML certificate.\n\nKey information:\n- Certificate number: R51/2006-GB1-09.04, Revision 2\n- Member State: United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland\n- Issuing authority: National Measurement Office\n- Person responsible: Paul Dixon – Product Certification Manager\n- Signatory: P R Dixon, for Chief Executive\n- Applicant: Thermo Ramsey Italia S.R.L., Strada Rivoltana km 6/7 Rodano (MI), 20090, Italy\n- Manufacturer: The applicant\n- Certified type: VersaWeigh, VersaGP, Versa RxC, Versa RxM and Teorema Checkweighers — Category X automatic checkweighers\n- Recommendation: OIML R 51 - Edition 2006(E), accuracy class XIII(1)\n- Issue Date: 28 July 2014\n- Reference No: T1108/0053\n- Pattern evaluation report P01088, 12 pages\n- Page 1 says \"This certificate includes 7 pages\" → page_total = 7\n\nTechnical data table 1 (6 columns):\n- Max: 1200 g, 2400 g, 4800 g (OCR \"48 00 g\" — that's clearly 4800 g), 12000 g, 24000 g, 48000 g\n- Min: 35 g, 70 g, 140 g, 350 g, 700 g, 1400 g\n- Scale interval: ≥0.5 g, ≥1 g, ≥2 g, ≥5 g, ≥10 g, ≥20 g\n- Max number of scale intervals: 2400 (all)\n- Load cell Emax: 10 kg, 20 kg, 30 kg, 100 kg, 100 or 200 kg, 100 or 300 kg\n- Tare: -500 e (single-range), -500 e1 (multi-range)\n- Belt speed: 1.2 m/s\n- Climatic environment: 0°C to +40°C, non-condensing (closed)\n- Power supply: 115-250 V a.c. 50 Hz\n- Accuracy class: XIII(1)\n\nOther Emax values may be chosen provided sensitivity > 1 µV/e.\n\nLoad cell: Tedea Huntleigh 1042 or 1250 C3, capacity according to table.\n\nInterfaces: RS232, USB, Ethernet, Parallel.\n\nDevices: list.\n\nSoftware: V312 Version 37.00.01.xx, MID Module.\n\nAlternatives: VersaGP different construction; Slack Conveyor with Vishay Celtron LPS load cell.\n\nSecond table (5 columns) — for the Slack Conveyor variant perhaps:\n- Max: 300, 300, 300, 600, 1500 g\n- Min: 4.5 g, 10 g, 10 g, 20 g, 50 g\n- Scale interval: ≥0.1 g, ≥0.2 g, ≥0.5 g, ≥0.2 g, ≥0.5 g\n- Load cell Emax: 2 kg, 2/3 kg, 2-10 kg, 3 kg, 10 kg\n\nHmm, the second table follows \"The instruments have the following technical characteristics.\" after the Slack Conveyor alternative description. This is ambiguous — it may be the capacity table for the Slack Conveyor weighing system (\"capacity according to table below\"). So the second table likely belongs to the Slack Conveyor alternative with Vishay Celtron LPS load cell.\n\nModel handling: The certificate covers multiple models. The main table has 6 variants based on Max capacity. The second table has 5 variants. How to model?\n\nI'll create model_family with models based on max capacity for table 1 (6 models) and table 2 (5 models). But the model_id should be distinguishable. For table 1: models like \"Max=1200g\" etc. For table 2 (Slack conveyor): \"Slack Conveyor Max=300g\" etc.\n\nAlternatively, use matrix_tables for both tables and model_level characteristics.\n\nLet me structure:\n\ncertificate:\n- number: R51/2006-GB1-09.04\n- scheme: The schema says scheme (A|B) — this is for OIML-CS basic/mid scheme. R51 certificate issued by NMO in 2014 under the older system, not really A or B. But schema asks scheme (A|B). Hmm. This is a pre-OIML-CS certificate? The OIML-CS started 2017. This cert is from 2014 with revision. I could omit scheme since it's not stated. Rule 8: don't fabricate. Omit.\n- project_number: T1108/0053 (Reference No). Actually \"Reference No: T1108/0053\" — that could be the NMO reference. I'll put project_number: \"T1108/0053\".\n- page_total: 7\n- member_state: \"United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland\" — maybe just \"United Kingdom\". I'll use the full text as stated.\n- date_issued: 2014-07-28\n- oiml_issuer_id: not present — omit.\n\nissuing_authority:\n- name: National Measurement Office\n- address_lines: [\"Stanton Avenue\", \"Teddington\", \"TW11 0JZ\", \"United Kingdom\"]\n- person_responsible: \"Paul Dixon – Product Certification Manager\" — or split: person_responsible: \"Paul Dixon\", person_title: \"Product Certification Manager\". The schema has person_responsible and person_title separately. \"Person responsible: Paul Dixon – Product Certification Manager\". I'll set person_responsible: \"Paul Dixon\", person_title: \"Product Certification Manager\".\n- phone: \"+44 (0)20 8943 7272\"\n- website: \"www.gov.uk/nmo\"\n- email: not present.\n\napplicants[]: name: Thermo Ramsey Italia S.R.L., address_lines: [\"Strada Rivoltana km 6/7 Rodano (MI)\", \"20090\", \"Italy\"]. Actually address format: \"Strada Rivoltana km 6/7 Rodano (MI), 20090, Italy\". I'll keep the lines.\n\nmanufacturers[]: name: \"Thermo Ramsey Italia S.R.L.\" (the applicant). address same as applicant. The cert says \"Manufacturer: The applicant\". I'll record name with a note? The schema doesn't have notes. I'll just set name to \"Thermo Ramsey Italia S.R.L.\" and address_lines same. Hmm, but \"The applicant\" — maybe record name as \"The applicant (Thermo Ramsey Italia S.R.L.)\"? Better: name: \"Thermo Ramsey Italia S.R.L.\" since manufacturer = applicant.\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Checkweigher\" or \"Automatic checkweighing instrument\". Schema examples: \"Load cell\", \"Indicator\", \"Measuring system\", \"Fuel dispenser\", \"Taximeter\", \"Gas meter\". For R51: \"Checkweigher\". I'll use \"Automatic checkweighing instrument\" — hmm. Category X automatic checkweigher. I'll put \"Checkweigher\".\n- type_designations: [\"VersaWeigh\", \"VersaGP\", \"Versa RxC\", \"Versa RxM\", \"Teorema\"]\n- module_designation: not applicable — not stated; omit or null. The cert covers complete instruments. I'll omit.\n- description: \"Mains-powered automatic checkweighing instrument ... operate as automatic checkweighers (Category X).\"\n\nmodel_family: The cert covers 5 type designations but the tables are by capacity. The first table has 6 capacity variants; second table (Slack conveyor) has 5 variants. The mapping between type designations (VersaWeigh, VersaGP, Versa RxC, Versa RxM, Teorema) and capacity columns is not given explicitly. The columns likely correspond to capacities within each type family.\n\nI think best approach: models keyed by capacity. Table 1: model_id \"Max=1200g\", \"Max=2400g\", \"Max=4800g\", \"Max=12000g\", \"Max=24000g\", \"Max=48000g\". Table 2 (Slack conveyor): model_id \"Slack Conveyor Max=300g\" (x3 columns but with different Min and e values — the three 300g columns differ in Min and scale interval, so they're distinct variants), \"Slack Conveyor Max=600g\", \"Slack Conveyor Max=1500g\".\n\nHmm, for table 2, columns 1-3 all have Max 300 g but differ in Min (4.5, 10, 10 g) and e (≥0.1, ≥0.2, ≥0.5). So there are 5 distinct variants. Model IDs could be \"Max=300g (e≥0.1g)\", \"Max=300g (e≥0.2g)\", \"Max=300g (e≥0.5g)\", \"Max=600g\", \"Max=1500g\". With prefix for slack conveyor.\n\nActually wait — is the second table for the Slack Conveyor alternative only, or is it a second set of characteristics for the instruments overall? The text: \"Having the instrument using the Slack Conveyor, non-powered weighing system... The load cell type may be as follows: Vishay Celtron LPS, capacity according to table below.\" Then page 4 starts, then \"- The characteristics... (Vishay Celtron LPS, capacity 2 kg)...\" then \"The instruments have the following technical characteristics.\" followed by the table. So yes, the second table is the capacity table for the Slack Conveyor variant (\"table below\" referenced). It applies to the Slack Conveyor weighing system used with Versa Weigh and Versa GP.\n\nSo the model family: The certificate covers multiple variants. I'll use matrix_tables for both tables and model_level characteristics.\n\nLet me structure matrix_tables:\n\nTable 1:\nname: \"Technical data\"\ncolumns: [\"maximum_capacity\", \"minimum_capacity\", \"scale_interval\", \"maximum_number_of_scale_intervals\", \"load_cell_emax\"]\ncolumn_units: [\"g\", \"g\", \"g\", null, \"kg\"]\nrows:\n- {maximum_capacity: 1200, minimum_capacity: 35, scale_interval: \"≥0.5\", maximum_number_of_scale_intervals: 2400, load_cell_emax: \"10 kg\"} — hmm, rows values should be numbers where possible. Let me do rows with units embedded in column names.\n\nMaybe simpler: rows: [{\"maximum_capacity_g\": 1200, \"minimum_capacity_g\": 35, \"scale_interval_g\": \"≥ 0.5\", \"max_scale_intervals\": 2400, \"load_cell_emax_kg\": \"10\"}, ...]. But the schema example for matrix_tables shows columns with units in column_units and rows with plain values, values can be strings for \"≥ 0.5\" style.\n\nFollowing the R137 example:\n```\n\"columns\": [\"size\", \"qmax\", \"qt\", \"qmin\", ...],\n\"column_units\": [null, \"m³/h\", ...],\n\"rows\": [{\"size\": \"G2.5\", \"qmax\": 4.0, ...}]\n```\n\nSo for table 1:\ncolumns: [\"maximum_capacity\", \"minimum_capacity\", \"scale_interval\", \"maximum_number_of_scale_intervals\", \"load_cell_emax\"]\ncolumn_units: [\"g\", \"g\", \"g\", null, \"kg\"]\nrows:\n1. {maximum_capacity: 1200, minimum_capacity: 35, scale_interval: 0.5 (min), ...}\n\nThe scale interval \"≥ 0.5 g\" — value {min: 0.5, max: null}? In a table row, simpler to keep as number with note, or as string \"≥0.5\". I could keep rows simple: scale_interval: 0.5 and add description that scale intervals are minimum values (≥). Or use {\"min\":0.5,\"max\":null}. Hmm, rows in example are plain scalars. I'll use plain numbers for scale_interval and note in description \"scale interval is a minimum (≥)\". Actually to be safe I can use strings \"≥ 0.5\" — but rules say numbers not strings. The structured value rules apply to characteristics; matrix rows... The example uses plain numbers. I'll use plain numbers and describe.\n\nLoad cell Emax values: \"10\", \"20\", \"30\", \"100\", \"100 or 200\", \"100 or 300\" — strings for the OR ones. I'll use strings: \"10\", \"20\", \"30\", \"100\", \"100 or 200\", \"100 or 300\". Or numbers where single. Mixed types are fine.\n\nTable 2 (Slack conveyor):\ncolumns: [\"maximum_capacity\", \"minimum_capacity\", \"scale_interval\", \"load_cell_emax\"]\ncolumn_units: [\"g\", \"g\", \"g\", \"kg\"]\nrows:\n1. {maximum_capacity: 300, minimum_capacity: 4.5, scale_interval: 0.1, load_cell_emax: \"2\"}\n2. {maximum_capacity: 300, minimum_capacity: 10, scale_interval: 0.2, load_cell_emax: \"2 or 3\"}\n3. {maximum_capacity: 300, minimum_capacity: 10, scale_interval: 0.5, load_cell_emax: \"2 to 10\"}\n4. {maximum_capacity: 600, minimum_capacity: 20, scale_interval: 0.2, load_cell_emax: \"3\"}\n5. {maximum_capacity: 1500, minimum_capacity: 50, scale_interval: 0.5, load_cell_emax: \"10\"}\n\nmodel_family models: For table 1, 6 models. For table 2, 5 models. Should these be in one model_family? The model_family covers cert-level models. I'll list all 11 variants, with labels distinguishing main vs slack conveyor.\n\nModel IDs:\nMain: \"Max=1200g\", \"Max=2400g\", \"Max=4800g\", \"Max=12000g\", \"Max=24000g\", \"Max=48000g\"\nSlack conveyor: \"SlackConveyor Max=300g e≥0.1g\", \"SlackConveyor Max=300g e≥0.2g\", \"SlackConveyor Max=300g e≥0.5g\", \"SlackConveyor Max=600g\", \"SlackConveyor Max=1500g\"\n\nmodel_level characteristics:\n- maximum_capacity (g): 11 values\n- minimum_capacity (g): 11 values\n- scale_interval (g): 11 values (min values)\n- load_cell_emax (kg): 11 values (strings for OR)\n\nType-level characteristics:\n- maximum_number_of_scale_intervals: 2400 (dimensionless)\n- tare: \"-500 e (single-range instruments), -500 e1 (multi-range instruments)\" — string value. Label: tare. value: \"-500 e (single-range), -500 e1 (multi-range)\". Hmm. Keep as string.\n- belt_speed: 1.2 m/s\n- climatic_environment: {min:0, max:40} °C — split: operating_temperature_range: {min:0,max:40}, unit °C; humidity: \"Non-condensing (closed)\". Label maybe \"temperature_range\" and \"environment: non-condensing (closed)\".\n- power_supply_voltage: {min:115, max:250} V\n- power_supply_frequency: 50 Hz\n- power_supply_type: \"AC\"\n- accuracy_class: \"XIII(1)\" — though R51 accuracy classes are X, XIII(1)? Hmm, \"accuracy class XIII(1)\" — that's odd. R51 accuracy classes are X, XI, XII, XIII (with 1 indicating... actually for automatic checkweighers classes are X, XI, XII, XIII and class mark can have factor like XIII(1)). The (1) here is actually a footnote? \"accuracy class XIII(1)\" — with footnote (1)? The markdown shows **XIII(1)** and the footnote isn't in the fragment. Hmm, R51 class notation: \"XIII(1)\" where (1) is the class factor... Actually in R51-1, accuracy classes are X, XI, XII, XIII, and the number in parentheses indicates the permitted error expressed in % ... Actually for automatic gravimetric filling instruments (R61) classes are X(1), X(2), etc. For checkweighers R51, classes X, XI, XII, XIII with subdivision like XIII(1)? Hmm. R51-1: checkweigher accuracy classes are X, XI, XII, XIII, and each may have \"class division\" in parentheses? Actually I recall R51 class marks: X(1), X(2)... no wait.\n\nOIML R51 (Automatic catchweighing instruments): accuracy classes XI, XII, XIII, XIII(1)? Hmm. Actually I recall checkweigher classes: XI, XII, XIII, XIII(1)? No — I think it's: class XI, XII, XIII, XIII.5? Let me think. R51: \"Checkweighers are classified into accuracy classes XI, XII, XIII and XIII(1)\"? Hmm not sure. R61 (gravimetric filling): X(1), X(2), X(5), X(10)... R51: accuracy classes X, XI, XII, XIII, XIII(1)? I'm not certain, but the certificate says XIII(1). I'll just record accuracy_class: \"XIII(1)\" as given, and also in recommendation.accuracy_classes: [\"XIII(1)\"].\n\nAlso \"Class X\" mentioned: \"operate as automatic checkweighers (Category X)\". Category X. Hmm — R51 category X means \"checkweigher for non-labeled products\"? Actually R51 has categories X and Y: X = checkweighers, Y = catchweighers used as... Category X instruments are for non-prepackaged? R51-1: instruments in category X (checkweighers for goods with... ) Actually: \"Category X: catchweighing instruments... used to check the weight of items with respect to a nominal weight; Category Y: ...\". Hmm, whatever. I'll include in description.\n\n- sensitivity condition: \"Other Emax values may be chosen provided the sensitivity is greater than 1 µV/e.\" → characteristic sensitivity: {min:1, max:null} unit \"µV/e\". Label: sensitivity.\n\ncomponents[]: Load cell component:\n- role: \"load cell\", type_designations: [\"Tedea Huntleigh 1042 C3\", \"Tedea Huntleigh 1250 C3\"], alternatives: \"OR\", characteristics: {...}. Also alternative load cell Vishay Celtron LPS for slack conveyor (capacity 2 kg tested).\n\nActually \"Tedea Huntleigh 1042 or 1250 C3\" — 1042 or 1250, both C3. And Vishay Celtron LPS.\n\ncomponents:\n1. {role: \"load cell\", type_designations: [\"Tedea Huntleigh 1042\", \"Tedea Huntleigh 1250\"], alternatives: \"OR\", characteristics: {accuracy_class? C3, ...}}. Also note \"capacity according to above table\".\n2. {role: \"load cell (Slack Conveyor alternative)\", type_designations: [\"Vishay Celtron LPS\"]}.\n\nMaybe combine with a note. I'll do two entries with descriptions in role.\n\nSoftware: general software V312 Version 37.00.01.xx, legally relevant module MID Module. Record as characteristics type_level:\n- general_software: \"V312 Version 37.00.01.xx\"\n- legally_relevant_software_module: \"MID Module\"\n\nInterfaces: interfaces: [\"RS232\", \"USB\", \"Ethernet\", \"Parallel\"] — could be characteristic or... schema doesn't have interfaces field. Could put in type_level characteristics: interfaces: value \"RS232, USB, Ethernet, Parallel\". I'll do that.\n\nDevices: list — could be a characteristic \"devices\" with list. Maybe skip or include as description string. I'll include a characteristic \"devices\" with value string listing them? That's noisy. I might include \"zero_setting_device\": true etc. Hmm. Keep it light: include devices as one characteristic with a string, or omit. The schema doesn't require completeness of everything; but \"Don't fabricate\" — including as string is fine. I'll include devices as type_level characteristic with string value listing them.\n\ntest_reports[]:\n- id: \"P01088\", role: \"type_evaluation_report\" (pattern evaluation report), pages: 12. Date not given.\n\nrecommendation:\n- id: \"R51\", edition: 2006, accuracy_classes: [\"XIII(1)\"]. Amendment not given. Scheme: not stated (pre-CS) — omit.\n\nrevision_history[]:\n- revision: \"2\", date: \"2014-07-28\" (issue date of this revision), changes: \"This revision replaces previous versions of the certificate.\"\n\nfootnotes[]: none defined in fragment. The \"(1)\" in XIII(1) is not a footnote necessarily. Skip footnotes.\n\ncharacteristics.type_level:\n- maximum_number_of_scale_intervals_n: 2400 (label \"maximum_number_of_scale_intervals_n\", unit null)\n- tare: value string \"-500 e (single-range instruments); -500 e1 (multi-range instruments)\" — or \"-500 e\" with note. I'll do value: {\"single_range\": \"-500 e\", \"multi_range\": \"-500 e1\"}? Value must be number|minmax|string. String: \"-500 e (single-range instruments), -500 e1 (multi-range instruments)\".\n- belt_speed: 1.2, \"m/s\"\n- temperature_range: {min:0, max:40}, \"°C\"\n- environment: \"Non-condensing (closed)\"\n- power_supply_voltage: {min:115, max:250}, \"V\"\n- power_supply_frequency: 50, \"Hz\"\n- power_supply_type: \"AC\"\n- sensitivity: {min:1, max:null}, \"µV/e\" — label \"sensitivity\" with description... \"Other Emax values may be chosen provided the sensitivity is greater than 1 µV/e\" → minimum_sensitivity: {min:1,max:null} unit µV/e. I'll label \"sensitivity\" value {min:1,max:null}.\n- interfaces: \"RS232, USB, Ethernet, Parallel\" (string)\n- accuracy_class: \"XIII(1)\"\n- general_software: \"V312 Version 37.00.01.xx\"\n- legally_relevant_software: \"MID Module\"\n\nHmm, accuracy class also in recommendation. Fine.\n\ndate_issued: \"2014-07-28\".\n\ncertificate.number: \"R51/2006-GB1-09.04\". scheme: omit. project_number: \"T1108/0053\" — hmm, is that a project number? \"Reference No: T1108/0053\" — it's NMO's certificate reference/test report reference. The test report is P01088. T1108/0053 could be the NMO internal reference. I'll put it in project_number. Or omit. I'll include it as project_number since it fits best.\n\npage_total: 7.\n\nmember_state: \"United Kingdom\".\n\nNow model_level characteristics. 11 models:\n\nmodels list:\nMain table:\n- {\"model_id\": \"Max=1200g\", \"label\": \"Maximum capacity 1200 g\", \"attributes\": {\"load_cell_emax\": \"10 kg\"}}\nHmm, attributes per model? Could duplicate. Keep simple: model_id only, plus attributes maybe minimal.\n\nLet me define models with attributes:\n1. {model_id: \"Max=1200g\", attributes: {minimum_capacity_g: 35, scale_interval_min_g: 0.5, load_cell_emax_kg: 10}}\nThat duplicates model_level. Maybe fine to keep models bare: {\"model_id\": \"Max=1200g\"}.\n\nI'll do:\n\nmodel_family:\n- family_name: null\n- models: 11 entries with model_id and label.\n\nmodel_level:\n- maximum_capacity, unit g, values for all 11\n- minimum_capacity, unit g, values for all 11\n- scale_interval (minimum), unit g, values: 0.5, 1, 2, 5, 10, 20 for main; 0.1, 0.2, 0.5, 0.2, 0.5 for slack. Use value as {min: x, max: null}? Simpler: value x with note in attribute name \"scale_interval_min\" hmm. The table says \"Scale interval (e =): ≥ 0.5 g\". I'll name attribute \"minimum_scale_interval\" and value numeric.\n\nWait — but per rules, ≥1000 → {min:1000, max:null}. For model_level values, the example R137 uses plain numbers. I'll use plain numbers and label \"minimum_scale_interval\". Good.\n\n- load_cell_emax, unit kg, values strings: \"10\",\"20\",\"30\",\"100\",\"100 or 200\",\"100 or 300\",\"2\",\"2 or 3\",\"2 to 10\",\"3\",\"10\".\n\nConfig-level: none clear.\n\nNow, main model ids: use \"Max=1200g\" etc. For slack conveyor: \"SlackConveyor_Max=300g_e>=0.1g\" — hmm avoid weird chars. \"Slack conveyor Max=300g (e≥0.1g)\". JSON fine with unicode.\n\nLet me finalize the JSON.\n\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R51/2006-GB1-09.04\",\n    \"revision\": 2? — schema doesn't have revision at certificate level. revision_history covers it. Omit.\n    \"project_number\": \"T1108/0053\",\n    \"page_total\": 7,\n    \"member_state\": \"United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland\",\n    \"date_issued\": \"2014-07-28\"\n  },\n  \"issuing_authority\": {\n    \"name\": \"National Measurement Office\",\n    \"address_lines\": [\"Stanton Avenue\", \"Teddington\", \"TW11 0JZ\", \"United Kingdom\"],\n    \"person_responsible\": \"Paul Dixon\",\n    \"person_title\": \"Product Certification Manager\",\n    \"phone\": \"+44 (0)20 8943 7272\",\n    \"website\": \"www.gov.uk/nmo\"\n  },\n  \"applicants\": [\n    {\"name\": \"Thermo Ramsey Italia S.R.L.\", \"address_lines\": [\"Strada Rivoltana km 6/7\", \"Rodano (MI)\", \"20090\", \"Italy\"]}\n  ],\n  \"manufacturers\": [\n    {\"name\": \"Thermo Ramsey Italia S.R.L. (the applicant)\", \"address_lines\": [...]}\n  ],\n  ...\n\nFor manufacturer name, better: \"Thermo Ramsey Italia S.R.L.\" with address same. Fine.\n\ncertified_type:\n  category: \"Automatic checkweighing instrument\"\n  type_designations: [\"VersaWeigh\", \"VersaGP\", \"Versa RxC\", \"Versa RxM\", \"Teorema\"]\n  module_designation: null — omit? Schema allows null. I'll set null since complete instrument, no module designation. Actually rule 8: omit if not present. The field \"module_designation\" — cert doesn't mention modules (except \"declaration of compatibility of modules\" generically and \"MID Module\" software). I'll omit module_designation.\n  description: \"Mains-powered automatic checkweighing instrument designated the VersaWeigh, VersaGP, Versa RxC, Versa RxM and Teorema Checkweighers, operating as automatic checkweighers (Category X).\"\n\nmodel_family: as above.\n\ncomponents:\n1. {role: \"load cell\", type_designations: [\"Tedea Huntleigh 1042 C3\", \"Tedea Huntleigh 1250 C3\"], alternatives: \"OR\", characteristics: {type_level: {\"capacity\": \"according to maximum capacity table\"}}}. Hmm, components characteristics shape — schema says components[]: role, type_designations[], alternatives, characteristics{}. Keep simple:\n   {\"role\": \"load cell (main constructions)\", \"type_designations\": [\"Tedea Huntleigh 1042 C3\", \"Tedea Huntleigh 1250 C3\"], \"alternatives\": \"OR\", \"characteristics\": {\"requirements\": \"R60 certificate required; capacity according to table; not a load cell with digital output; nlc, Y, Z same or better than Tedea 1042 C3 10 kg; same design and material; no oil damper\"}}\n   \n   Hmm characteristics{} — free object. I'll keep it minimal: maybe omit characteristics and add nothing. But capacity reference is useful. I'll include characteristics: {\"capacity\": \"according to maximum capacity table\", \"reference_tested_load_cell\": \"Tedea Huntleigh 1042 C3, 10 kg\"}.\n\n2. {\"role\": \"load cell (Slack Conveyor alternative)\", \"type_designations\": [\"Vishay Celtron LPS\"], \"characteristics\": {\"capacity\": \"according to Slack Conveyor capacity table\", \"reference_tested_load_cell\": \"Vishay Celtron LPS, 2 kg\"}}\n\ntest_reports:\n[{\"id\": \"P01088\", \"pages\": 12, \"role\": \"type_evaluation_report\"}]\n\nrecommendation:\n{\"id\": \"R51\", \"edition\": 2006, \"accuracy_classes\": [\"XIII(1)\"]}\n\nNote: \"OIML R 51 - Edition 2006(E)\". edition 2006.\n\nrevision_history:\n[{\"revision\": \"2\", \"date\": \"2014-07-28\", \"changes\": \"This revision replaces previous versions of the certificate.\"}]\n\ncharacteristics:\n\ntype_level:\n- maximum_number_of_scale_intervals_n: {value: 2400}\n- tare: {value: \"-500 e (single-range instruments); -500 e1 (multi-range instruments)\"}\n- belt_speed: {value: 1.2, unit_symbol: \"m/s\"}\n- temperature_range: {value: {min:0, max:40}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}\n- environment_humidity: {value: \"Non-condensing (closed)\"} — label \"climatic_environment\": \"Non-condensing (closed)\". I'll do two: temperature_range and \"climate\": \"Non-condensing (closed)\".\n- power_supply_voltage: {value: {min:115, max:250}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n- power_supply_frequency: {value: 50, unit_symbol: \"Hz\"}\n- power_supply_type: {value: \"AC\"}\n- minimum_sensitivity: {value: {min:1, max:null}, unit_symbol: \"µV/e\"} — with description? Add footnote? No footnote system here; I'll label \"sensitivity\" and rely on... hmm the context: \"Other Emax values may be chosen provided the sensitivity is greater than 1 µV/e\". Value: \"greater than 1 µV/e\" → {min:1,max:null} is ≥, but text says \"greater than\" — close enough. unit \"µV/e\".\n- interfaces: {value: [\"RS232\",\"USB\",\"Ethernet\",\"Parallel\"]} — value must be number|minmax|string. Use string \"RS232, USB, Ethernet, Parallel\".\n- accuracy_class: {value: \"XIII(1)\"} — also \"Category X\". Add category? It's in certified_type description. Maybe skip.\n- general_software_version: {value: \"V312 Version 37.00.01.xx\"}\n- legally_relevant_software_module: {value: \"MID Module\"}\n\nmodel_level: as described.\n\nLet me build values arrays:\n\nmaximum_capacity (g):\n- Max=1200g: 1200\n- Max=2400g: 2400\n- Max=4800g: 4800\n- Max=12000g: 12000\n- Max=24000g: 24000\n- Max=48000g: 48000\n- SlackConveyor Max=300g (e≥0.1g): 300\n- SlackConveyor Max=300g (e≥0.2g): 300\n- SlackConveyor Max=300g (e≥0.5g): 300\n- SlackConveyor Max=600g: 600\n- SlackConveyor Max=1500g: 1500\n\nminimum_capacity (g): 35, 70, 140, 350, 700, 1400, 4.5, 10, 10, 20, 50\n\nminimum_scale_interval (g): 0.5, 1, 2, 5, 10, 20, 0.1, 0.2, 0.5, 0.2, 0.5\n\nload_cell_emax (kg): \"10\",\"20\",\"30\",\"100\",\"100 or 200\",\"100 or 300\",\"2\",\"2 or 3\",\"2 to 10\",\"3\",\"10\"\n\nModel ids — use \"Versa Max=1200 g\"? The types don't map 1:1 to capacities. I'll use neutral ids.\n\nModel IDs: \"Max=1200g\", \"Max=2400g\", \"Max=4800g\", \"Max=12000g\", \"Max=24000g\", \"Max=48000g\", \"SlackConveyor Max=300g e>=0.1g\"... I'll use \"Max=300g (Slack Conveyor, e≥0.1g)\" etc. Slightly verbose but clear. Use unicode ≥ fine.\n\nmatrix_tables: two entries as above.\n\nFootnotes: none.\n\nAlso note \"Maximum capacity (Max): 1 200 g\" — the \"48 00 g\" is OCR of 4 800 g → 4800. Yes.\n\nBelt speed 1.2 m/s.\n\nNow write final JSON. Keep units consistent.\n\nOne more: \"certificate.scheme\" — omit (not A/B scheme cert).\n\nOutput single JSON, no fences.",
